    {
      "question": "e-9126",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "d2539bf7e58ae90175f39380c0a207d458e980e1c83f31820afe2bd0029aeade",
      "new_sha256": "8b3835500959de660f29095e515e368b4c7632cac00960d3ed2b6cc2321683a5",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-9126",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The support of $X$ is $S_X = \\{0,1\\},$ the support of $Y$ is $S_Y = \\{0,1\\},$ and therefore the joint support $S$ is\n\n$$\nS= S_X\\times S_Y = \\left\\{(0,0),  (0,1),  (1,0),  (1,1) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a joint probability distribution $f(x,y),$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0)+f(0,1)+f(1,0)+f(1,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{a}{2}+a+a+\\frac{5}{2}a & =1 \\\\ 5a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061806/example-9126.json",
          "file_sha256": "83f37d13971d1d37ec7e30988c3313f61caf3d4e510265e73b44fceaee237569"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 1973,
